{
  "result": "At the end of my street there is a small park that nobody seems to notice. It has two benches, one crooked tree, and a patch of grass that turns brown every summer. Yet I think it may be the most honest place in the neighborhood.\n\nPeople arrive there without announcing themselves. A delivery driver eats lunch beneath the tree. Two children argue about the rules of a game they have just invented. An elderly man sits with a newspaper, reading perhaps three lines before watching the pigeons instead. Nobody asks the park to be remarkable. It simply makes room.\n\nI used to believe that a meaningful life required a dramatic story: a great achievement, a sudden discovery, a journey across an ocean. Those things can matter, of course. But the park suggests another possibility. Meaning might also accumulate quietly, through repeated acts of attention: remembering a neighbor\u2019s name, sharing an umbrella, or giving someone enough silence to finish a difficult thought.\n\nOne morning, after a storm, I found a woman gathering fallen branches from the path. She had no uniform and carried no official tools. When I offered to help, she smiled and handed me a handful of twigs.\n\nWe worked for ten minutes without exchanging names. By the time we finished, the path was clear and the sun had appeared. The park looked almost exactly as it had before the storm.\n\nAlmost. There was one small difference: I knew that someone else cared for it, just like me.",
